# rules: swift-accepts-any-certificate
# languages: swift
# fixture: swift/CWE-295.swift
# from: CWE-295 (Improper Certificate Validation), CWE-297, MASVS-NETWORK-1
#
# `URLCredential(trust:)` wraps the chain the server has just sent and offers
# it back as the credential to go on with. Built from the challenge's own
# `serverTrust` and handed to the completion handler with nothing in between,
# it is a TLS check that answers yes to every certificate - which is every
# proxy on every network the phone joins.
#
# The safe spellings all leave something in the method to see: an evaluation
# (`SecTrustEvaluateWithError`), or a comparison of the leaf against a
# certificate the app shipped, which reads as `SecTrustCopyCertificateChain`
# and `SecCertificateCopyData`. So the question this rule asks is the whole
# method's text, not the call:
#
#   the method builds a credential out of a trust object
#   and nothing anywhere in it evaluates or reads that trust
#
# That is coarser than a structural test and coarse in the quiet direction,
# which is the same trade `kotlin-accepts-any-hostname` makes and for the same
# reason: a rule that cannot tell a good comparison from a bad one has no
# business reporting either, and what it *can* tell is the absence of one. A
# method that evaluates one trust and accepts another goes unreported.
#
# `URLCredential(identity:certificates:persistence:)` is client-certificate
# authentication and is not this - the app is proving who it is, not deciding
# who the server is - so keying on the `trust:` label rather than on the type
# leaves it alone by construction.
#
# The method scaffold is `func $F() { $$$BODY }`, and neither half of that
# spelling says what it looks like it says. The `()` compiles to nothing at
# all, so the pattern reaches a function of any arity - which is just as well,
# because `func $F($$$_)` is not code in Swift: a parameter there is `name:
# Type` and an ellipsis is neither. And the body hole has to be variadic.
# `{ $BODY }` anchors to the body's only child, and tree-sitter-swift hangs a
# comment off the body beside the statements, so the singular spelling silently
# skips every method with a comment in it - which in this fixture is every
# method the rule is about. See GUIDE.md.

["URLCredential(trust: $T)"] as $trusts
| ["func $F() { $$$BODY }"] as $methods
| "SecTrustEvaluate|SecTrustCopyCertificateChain|SecTrustGetCertificateAtIndex"
  + "|SecCertificateCopyData|SecPolicyCreateSSL|evaluate\\s*\\(" as $checksTheTrust
| scan_ast("*.swift"; $trusts + $methods) as $all
| ( $all | of($trusts)
    | within($all | of($methods) | where_capture_not("BODY"; $checksTheTrust)) )
| finding("swift-accepts-any-certificate";
    "this builds the credential out of the certificate the server just sent "
    + "and nothing in this method evaluates it, so any certificate is accepted")
| report
